      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers by \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) where \\( x > y \\).\n\nWe are given two pieces of information:\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6.\n2. The difference of their squares is 12.\n\nWe can express these pieces of information as two equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 6 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\)\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the first equation into this identity, we get:\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) = 6(x - y) \\]\n\nWe know from the second piece of information that \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\), so we can substitute this into the equation above:\n\\[ 6(x - y) = 12 \\]\n\nTo find \\( x - y \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 6:\n\\[ x - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2 \\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can find the values of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). From \\( x + y = 6 \\), we can express \\( x \\) as \\( x = 6 - y \\). Substituting this into the equation \\( x - y = 2 \\), we get:\n\\[ (6 - y) - y = 2 \\]\n\\[ 6 - 2y = 2 \\]\n\\[ -2y = 2 - 6 \\]\n\\[ -2y = -4 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\]\n\nThen, substituting \\( y = 2 \\) back into \\( x + y = 6 \\), we get:\n\\[ x + 2 = 6 \\]\n\\[ x = 4 \\]\n\nSo, the two numbers are \\( x = 4 \\) and \\( y = 2 \\). The positive difference is:\n\\[ x - y = 4 - 2 = 2 \\]\n\nThe solution is verified to be correct. The positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
