      "text": "To solve this problem, we can set up a system of equations based on the given information. Let's denote the two numbers as \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have the following two equations:\n\n1. \\( x + y = 6 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\)\n\nWe can use the difference of squares formula, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the value of \\( x + y \\) from the first equation into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) = 6(x - y) = 12 \\]\n\nNow, we can solve for \\( x - y \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 6:\n\n\\[ x - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2 \\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\( \\boxed{2} \\).\n\nTo verify, we can find the values of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) using the equations \\( x + y = 6 \\) and \\( x - y = 2 \\). Adding these two equations, we get:\n\n\\[ (x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 2x = 8 \\]\n\\[ x = 4 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x = 4 \\) back into the equation \\( x + y = 6 \\), we get:\n\n\\[ 4 + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\]\n\nSo, the two numbers are \\( 4 \\) and \\( 2 \\). The positive difference between these two numbers is:\n\n\\[ |4 - 2| = 2 \\]\n\nThis confirms that the positive difference of the two numbers is indeed \\( \\boxed{2} \\).",
